From d091911f4c68911c446991bea45ae82061ad4731 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jolyon Brown <jolyon@limilo.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 01:47:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix register storage class not accepted

The register keyword was not known to the scanner, so any declaration
using it (register int i; or a register parameter) failed with
error 3005 "Identifier not defined 'register'". C89 3.5.1 / C99 6.7.1
make register a storage-class specifier whose allocation hint may be
ignored.

Add a TK_REGISTER token next to TK_EXTERN, map the word to it in the
scanner, and have the parser accept and ignore it the way it ignores
typename: as a prefix in ParseBaseTypeDeclaration (locals, parameters,
const register) and in the token lists that decide a declaration
starts (ParseSimpleExpression, ParseStatement, IsTypeToken). The
keyword is accepted and ignored in C++ as well. No code generation
changes: the regression test compiled with the keyword and the same
file compiled on main with -dregister= give byte-identical output in
all ten autotest configurations.

Because the word is now a keyword, #pragma register(name, address)
no longer arrives as an identifier; ParsePragma handles TK_REGISTER
first with the existing pragma body. Placing the token inside the
TK_IF..TK_ASM range keeps #define register <body> a valid directive
(the body is no longer left in the token stream); like every other
keyword, such a macro is recorded under the token name and never
expands, so #define register static, which took effect while register
was an identifier, is now a no-op.

The regression test registerstoragetest.c covers register locals,
parameters of integer, pointer, struct pointer, long, float and
typedef'd types, const register, register volatile, a register pointer
with const, a prototype and function pointer type with a register
parameter, for-init and nested block scopes, and #pragma register.
After main, where they cannot hide the other uses from a preprocessor
that expands them, an empty #define register precedes a function with
register parameters, and an unused #define register ) checks that the
replacement list stays out of the program text.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
